WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:08.140
It’s impossible to know, but this is probably
Henry Ford’s head.

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:10.820
And this?

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:16.120
This is definitely Henry Ford washing up by
a stream.

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:20.720
Starting in 1915, these four men camped and
road tripped together, regularly:

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:21.890
Lemme show you.

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:26.290
You’ve got John Burroughs — imagine somebody
as famous as Bill Nye or Neil deGrasse Tyson,

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:28.030
but for nature.

00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:33.960
There’s tire magnate Harvey Firestone, Henry
Ford, founder of Ford Motor Company, and Thomas

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.640
Edison, America’s most famous inventor.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:42.059
This group’s adventures took two American
classics and brought them to a whole new level:

00:00:42.059 --> 00:00:49.609
The road trip and the publicity stunt, too.

00:00:49.609 --> 00:00:57.890
You could get lost in John Burroughs beard.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:02.499
Burroughs is the least likely of this road
trip group, but he’s kind of the linchpin.

00:01:02.499 --> 00:01:04.120
He was conservation elite.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:09.380
Here’s his beard and Teddy Roosevelt at
Yellowstone in 1903.

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:12.180
He knew the environmental implications of
industry.

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:17.490
When the Model T came out in 1908, he called
it a “demon on wheels.”

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:19.970
So Henry Ford sent him one.

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:21.960
They became friends.

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:26.950
This is John Burroughs inside his demon on
wheels.

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:32.020
Ford had long known Edison as his mentor,
and Firestone was a natural fourth from the

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:33.460
tire connection.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:39.719
The group started roadtripping together and
they called themselves “The Vagabonds.”

00:01:39.719 --> 00:01:42.490
Hijinks ensued.

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:44.890
Yep.

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:48.969
Henry Ford just climbed a tree.

00:01:48.969 --> 00:01:54.520
After driving over primitive roads, they communed
with nature, following a typical routine:

00:01:54.520 --> 00:02:05.740
Burroughs went for long walks, Firestone read,
Edison napped, and Henry Ford, well, he chopped

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:07.280
wood.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:11.210
He really hated wood.

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:15.470
Along their routes, they met other inventors
and business titans who occasionally tagged

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:16.470
along.

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:21.319
According to one essay by a fellow road-tripper,
Thomas Edison also called Firestone and his

00:02:21.319 --> 00:02:25.629
son Harvey Jr. “dudes” for an entire trip.

00:02:25.629 --> 00:02:29.599
Now the meaning at the time is a little bit
different, because “dude” might have been

00:02:29.599 --> 00:02:33.450
a city person, or a well-dressed person.

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:37.760
But let’s not lose site of the fact that
Thomas Edison was walking around calling Harvey

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:43.610
Firestone “dude” when he wanted to get
his attention.

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:45.340
They took a lot of trips.

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:50.370
This 1918 route was typical of their regular
journeys, and the group of “great men”

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:52.560
earned headlines everywhere they went.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:57.319
On this trip, they traveled throughout Virginia,
hit North Carolina, and zipped back up to

00:02:57.319 --> 00:02:58.319
Maryland.

00:02:58.319 --> 00:03:03.200
Their fun road trips were a phenomenon, like
here, when Asheville, North Carolina papers

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.459
covered their arrival, visit, and departure.

00:03:07.459 --> 00:03:09.410
It was called a “trip with friends.”

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:13.890
But it wasn’t just a normal road trip.

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:20.550
Here’s a secret.

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:24.409
When the most powerful men in the country
go on a road trip, they do it a little differently

00:03:24.409 --> 00:03:25.739
than everybody else.

00:03:25.739 --> 00:03:28.220
Honestly, this was glamping.

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:35.439
They brought along a film crew with big bulky
cameras, they had chefs, and five-star service

00:03:35.439 --> 00:03:37.520
the entire time.

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:40.140
This table?

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:43.650
It spins.

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:48.120
Besides delicious meals cooked in the open
air, these trips served a purpose.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:53.220
Newspapers reported on the Vagabonds as if
they actually were vagabonds.

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:59.329
They said the trip showed “a fellowship
with the common man and a governing concern

00:03:59.329 --> 00:04:02.180
for the little man’s interests and welfare.”

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:06.409
At the same time they were wrangling camera
crews to film how normal they were, they were

00:04:06.409 --> 00:04:09.189
also managing their business interests.

00:04:09.189 --> 00:04:14.590
That early 1916 trip included a meetup with
Edward N. Hurley, a businessman and the chair

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:20.519
of the United States Shipping Board - an emergency
Naval Agency established as the United States

00:04:20.519 --> 00:04:23.780
came closer to entering World War I.

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:27.970
In Pennsylvania, the power players conferred,
as one observer described it, to “divide

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:30.750
up the work ahead in manufacturing.”

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:32.450
World War One breaks out?

00:04:32.450 --> 00:04:38.521
Road trip!
Hurley came on future trips with the vagabonds

00:04:38.521 --> 00:04:39.521
as well.

00:04:39.521 --> 00:04:45.020
Ford also gobbled up land to explore steam
power and agricultural possibilities, and

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:48.680
the group met with agriculturalists and botanists.

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:54.590
In 1918, while on the road, Ford had been
drafted into a Senate campaign as well, which

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:56.270
he narrowly lost.

00:04:56.270 --> 00:04:59.180
We’ll never know everything they talked
about.

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:05.780
According to Burroughs, Ford shared his anti-semitic
conspiracy theories over late night chats.

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:11.050
The vagabonds were camping, but they were
also making deals and running a PR campaign,

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:16.880
highlighting each of the men's professional
interests and making them seem fun and accessible.

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:18.440
This is Henry Ford’s toast.

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:24.680
And that’s the thing they served up for
the public.

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:26.949
John Burroughs died in 1921.

00:05:26.949 --> 00:05:30.110
He was 83.

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:32.080
After his death, the trips continued.

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:34.110
That new guy, chopping wood?

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:36.320
It’s President Warren G. Harding.

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:42.509
Still, even by the standards of the vagabonds,
the publicity and entourage grew too big.

00:05:42.509 --> 00:05:45.180
The road trips stopped in 1924.

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:51.070
But the vagabonds had a lasting impact on
the American notion of the road trip and how

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:55.340
powerful people network, even when they’re
taking naps.

00:05:55.340 --> 00:06:01.140
So was it a publicity stunt or a real road
trip with buddies?

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:02.140
Maybe it was both.

00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:04.710
Or some third thing altogether.

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:07.840
This is a nearly hundred year-old campfire.

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:12.160
John Burroughs wrote an essay about the trip
and why he thought they took it.

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:17.880
Maybe it was even true:
“We grow weary of our luxuries and conveniences.

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:24.340
We react against our complex civilization,
and long to get back for a time to first principles.

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:32.480
We cheerfully endure wet, cold, smoke, mosquitoes,
black flies, and sleepless nights, just to

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:35.030
touch naked reality once more.”

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:41.919
Hey what’s up?

00:06:41.919 --> 00:06:45.910
That was the first episode of Almanac: Road
Trip Edition, and now we’re gonna be reading

00:06:45.910 --> 00:06:51.500
some comments and we’re gonna reveal the
name of this guy, which you all helped choose

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:54.520
in the comments to the trailer.

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:57.650
Reading comments on the internet — it’s
not a new thing — but I’m particularly

00:06:57.650 --> 00:06:59.729
inspired by Potato Jet.

00:06:59.729 --> 00:07:06.080
He is a camera and tech vlogger and he’s
just generally hilarious and really smart.

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:10.770
So please go check him out — he always does
this at the end of his videos and I love it,

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:12.450
so we’re gonna try it here.

00:07:12.450 --> 00:07:18.360
I gave everybody the challenge of naming this
wavy, floaty guy: top comment by far, Chris

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:22.669
Saunders says, “he has a name, it’s wacky
waving inflatable arm-flailing tube man.”

00:07:22.669 --> 00:07:24.870
If you don’t know, this is a Family Guy
reference.

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:27.280
“Wacky waving inflatable arm-flailing tube
man.”

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:32.039
Very funny, but I asked you all to name him,
not the writers for Family Guy, and you know

00:07:32.039 --> 00:07:36.080
I just would have called Seth McFarlane if
we needed that.

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:38.270
Second most popular comment is actually about
Route 66.

00:07:38.270 --> 00:07:43.629
It’s from Sebastian Elytron and he says,
“That’s what the world-famous Route 66

00:07:43.629 --> 00:07:44.629
looks like?

00:07:44.629 --> 00:07:46.539
I thought it was more glamorous.”

00:07:46.539 --> 00:07:51.160
In that trailer, I’m actually sitting on
Route 66 the whole time, so if you notice

00:07:51.160 --> 00:07:54.419
me, like, looking up a lot, it’s because
I’m worried I’m gonna be run over by a

00:07:54.419 --> 00:07:55.419
car.

00:07:55.419 --> 00:08:00.050
So there are really desolate sections like
that and then there are also unpaved sections

00:08:00.050 --> 00:08:02.990
which I also show in that trailer.

00:08:02.990 --> 00:08:08.349
You’ll learn the whole history and everything
about Route 66 in Episode four of this road

00:08:08.349 --> 00:08:09.349
trip edition.

00:08:09.349 --> 00:08:11.349
“The name of your friend: Lab Flute of Knowledge.”

00:08:11.349 --> 00:08:15.259
I have no idea what that means but thumbs
up to that.

00:08:15.259 --> 00:08:19.039
Hane Grace Yagel: “His name should be Al
Maniac, he seems like a wild one.”

00:08:19.039 --> 00:08:21.810
Alright, without further adieu let’s scroll
up to the winner.

00:08:21.810 --> 00:08:25.479
This is pretty much the second most popular
answer, it’s from ZmbrSys.

00:08:25.479 --> 00:08:26.479
“Mack!

00:08:26.479 --> 00:08:27.479
Rhymes with almanac.

00:08:27.479 --> 00:08:28.849
Lots of Mack trucks on the road.”

00:08:28.849 --> 00:08:34.010
It makes sense, it’s fun, and it’s easy
to fit it on screen, so gavel, Mack, that’s

00:08:34.010 --> 00:08:35.010
it.

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:39.840
There’s a desk down here by the way, but
the gavel was my fist, and it hurts.

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:41.340
Alright, so that’s it.

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:45.430
In the next episode, I will be answering any
questions or concerns that you have about

00:08:45.430 --> 00:08:52.610
the vagabonds from this video, so drop those
below, let me know, and until then, happy

00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:53.610
glamping.

00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:54.610
Yep.

00:08:54.610 --> 00:08:56.000
I said the word glamping again.

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:58.140
[Deep, sad sigh.]

